  "response": "그 상황에서 판단을 내려야 했는데, 판단을 내리기 위해 여러 사람에게 확인을 구했던 거잖아요. 그 자체가 무책임했던 건 아닌 것 같은데, 그 이후로 혼자 있을 때 소리에 몸이 굳는 반응이 생겼다는 건, 그때의 긴장이 아직 몸 안에 남아 있다는 신호일 수 있어요.\n\n\"예민한 건지 잘못된 건지\"를 구분하려는 마음이 느껴지는데, 사실 그 둘 사이에는 다른 가능성도 있어요. 그때 실제로 위험한 순간이었고, 몸이 \"다시 그런 일이 오면 안 된다\"고 경계 태세를 유지하고 있는 거라면, 예민함이라기보다는 일종의 학습된 반응에 가까울 수도 있으니까요.\n\n혼자 근무할 때 몸이 굳는 그 순간, 보통 어떤 생각이 먼저 떠오르세요?",
  "reasoning": "Let me analyze what the client is sharing:\n\n1. They work in a safety-critical environment (likely industrial/technical) where they had to respond to a sensor warning.\n2. During the incident, they couldn't decide whether to stop immediately, so they called multiple people, which delayed reporting.\n3. Fortunately, no actual accident occurred.\n4. Since then, they freeze at minor sounds during solo shifts.\n5. They're questioning whether this is \"just being too sensitive\" or something actually wrong.\n\nKey emotional themes:\n- Self-doubt about whether their experience is valid (\"is this even a problem?\")\n- Fear of judgment (calling it \"sensitive\" vs. something \"wrong\")\n- Hypervigilance/freeze response after a near-miss incident\n- Uncertainty about what happened during the incident (decision paralysis)\n\nWhat would be helpful here:\n- Validate that their experience is legitimate without being dismissive\n- Not rush to reassure (\"it's okay, nothing happened\") because that could minimize their experience\n- Acknowledge the freeze response as a understandable reaction to a high-stakes situation\n- Maybe gently reflect back what they're describing without pathologizing or normalizing too quickly\n- The client is stuck in a binary: \"too sensitive\" vs. \"something wrong\" - I could gently open that up without forcing a reframe\n\nI should:\n- Acknowledge the weight of what they described\n- Not dismiss the \"is this a problem\" question but also not confirm it as a disorder\n- Reflect the freeze response as something that makes sense given the context\n- Perhaps gently name what happened: a high-pressure moment where they had to make a judgment call with incomplete information, and now their body is on alert\n- Ask one question that helps move forward - maybe about what they need right now, or about the freeze response specifically\n\nLet me draft a response that's warm, validating, and gently opens up the \"sensitive vs. wrong\" binary without being preachy.\n",
